course: dictionary
lesson: 6070
title: Merriment: happy fun together
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Merriment means joyful fun, laughter and enjoyment, especially when people are together and celebrating. You will often find it at feasts, parties, Christmas and weddings. Look at the words around it to work out its meaning.
explain: «We all know Toad!»—(great laughter)—«Good Toad, modest Toad, honest Toad!» (shrieks of merriment). The great laughter and the shrieks show that merriment is noisy, happy fun shared by a group. Those clues tell you it is a good feeling.
explain: Merriment can also mean amusement, as in «his mistake caused much merriment», where people found the mistake funny. Phrases such as festive merriment and eyes twinkling with merriment show it too. Stories often use the word to show that everyone is happy.
fact: Merriment is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say much merriment, not a merriment.
fact: Merriment means happy fun, laughter and enjoyment, especially when people celebrate together.
fact: «Merriment» can also mean amusement, the feeling of finding something funny.
fact: «Festive merriment» means happy celebrating, such as at Christmas.
fact: Merriment is a little formal or old-fashioned, so you will meet it more in stories than in everyday chat.
exercise: «The party was full of merriment, with music and dancing.» What does merriment mean here? | Angry shouting | A kind of food | Sadness and quiet | *Happy fun and laughter
exercise: «His mistake caused much merriment.» What does merriment mean in this sentence? | Sleep | *Amusement | Worry | Anger
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to merriment? | Silence | Gloom | Worry | *Glee
test: In the story, the shrieks of merriment show that the friends were what? | Cross and bored | Tired and sleepy | *Happy and laughing together | Lost in the woods
test: Which sentence uses merriment correctly? | The rain brought merriment to the empty road | *The wedding guests filled the hall with merriment | The class sat in silent merriment | He ate his merriment at breakfast
exam: What does the word merriment tell the reader about the people in a story? | They are angry and hungry | They are asleep and tired | They are lost and frightened | *They are happy and laughing together
